# MODULE 7: RECEIPTS AND INVOICES

The system must distinguish between an F&B outlet receipt, a PMS folio, a payment receipt, and the final hotel invoice.

Do not treat them as interchangeable documents.

## A. DIRECT F&B PAYMENT RECEIPT

For a guest who pays at the restaurant, the receipt should include:

- Hotel name.
- Outlet name.
- Receipt number.
- F&B bill number.
- Date and time.
- Cashier.
- Items purchased.
- Quantities.
- Unit prices.
- Discounts.
- Service charge.
- Tax breakdown.
- Total bill.
- Payment method.
- Amount paid.
- Balance due.
- Transaction status.

The receipt should clearly indicate that payment was received.

## B. ROOM-CHARGED F&B RECEIPT

When the bill is charged to the guest's room, the receipt must not falsely indicate that the guest has paid the bill.

It should display:

- Hotel name.
- Outlet name.
- F&B receipt number.
- Bill number.
- Date and time.
- Guest name.
- Room number.
- Items purchased.
- Quantities.
- Item prices.
- Discounts.
- Service charge.
- Tax breakdown.
- Total bill.
- Settlement method: Charge to Room.
- PMS folio reference.
- Outstanding amount posted to the room.
- Status: Posted to Guest Folio / Payable at Front Desk.

The receipt should clearly communicate:

**This transaction has been charged to the guest's hotel account and is not a payment receipt.**

If the existing system supports an outlet-specific receipt format, preserve that design while ensuring these details are available.

## C. PARTIAL PAYMENT RECEIPT

For a split-settled transaction, display:

Original bill: ₦30,000

Cash received: ₦10,000

Charged to room: ₦20,000

Outstanding at Front Desk: ₦20,000

Settlement status: Partially Paid / Partially Posted to Guest Folio

The receipt must not show the complete bill as cash paid.

## D. FINAL HOTEL RECEIPT AND INVOICE

At checkout, the Front Desk should produce the appropriate final hotel invoice and payment receipt according to the existing PMS billing design and applicable legal requirements.

The consolidated invoice should identify:

- Accommodation.
- Restaurant charges.
- Bar charges.
- Room service.
- Laundry.
- Other billable services.
- Discounts.
- Applicable taxes.
- Total charges.
- Payments received.
- Remaining balance, if any.
- Final settlement status.

Do not issue duplicate financial invoices for the same taxable transaction.

The F&B outlet receipt and the final hotel invoice must remain linked through transaction references.

The tax engine must determine the appropriate invoice treatment.

